[
  "ACT 1: COLD OPEN & PERSONAL GROUNDING [00:00 - 03:00]\nScene: 01. Starting Soon -> 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nWelcome to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. This is Episode 0: Mike Hall on our Northern Illinois Field Dispatch under the high-tension wires. In October 2026, I walked out underneath 345,000-volt transmission lines in Northern Illinois with a smartphone to record a field dispatch on software craftsmanship and identity. When I checked the footage back at my desk, the audio sounded completely destroyed: deafening 60 Hz electromagnetic hum, corona arcing, and an apparent three-and-a-half-minute clock drift. Trashing the footage and reshooting is the standard advice. But working through that recording became a living case study in what happens when we lean on modern AI models, and why the bottleneck in engineering is never generation: it is verification.",
  "ACT 2: SETTING THE HISTORICAL SCENE [03:00 - 08:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nI started with a simple thirteen-word query in a browser, then brought it into Gemini Flash through the terminal. The model immediately leapt into action: generating cascaded Butterworth filter equations, calculating exact duration ratios, and asserting with supreme, effortless confidence that the audio clock had drifted by twenty percent. It gave me shell scripts, math, and complete DSP pipelines. And it was all totally wrong. In Hollywood, investigators yell 'Enhance!' and pixels appear out of thin air. In software, that wishful thinking produces hallucinated AI slop. It reminds me of the Dell Studio XPS laptop I carried during the 2009 Obtiva days, with the sticker that read: 'Typing is not the bottleneck.' In 2026, generating code is not the bottleneck either. The bottleneck is domain understanding.",
  "ACT 3: THE CORE INQUIRY: THE SOUND ABOVE [08:00 - 14:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera -> 04. Split Screen\n\nThe container reported 21 minutes of video and only 18 minutes of audio. The model assumed linear drift and handed me atempo=0.837. When I tested the audio in my headphones, it was slurred and completely out of sync. So we went back to ground truth: high-FPS frame extraction, Whisper subtitle alignment, and audio waveforms. What actually happened? At 18 minutes, I said 'Thank you' and wrapped the talk. The remaining three and a half minutes was just me walking with the camera running in silence. The speech was at 1.0x normal speed all along. When AI models project effortless competence, how do you verify where competence ends and plausible falsehood begins? You verify with empirical tests, not conversational trust.",
  "ACT 4: HALLWAY REWATCH & VIDEO REACTION [14:00 - 28:00]\nScene: 03. Reaction - Video Focus\n\nLet us look at the raw recording from the phone (YouTube ID: 7YeFpKn5FbU). Notice the stereo separation. Modern smartphones have dual microphones for noise suppression. The secondary microphone at the top was an unshielded inductive loop antenna, picking up 65 times more electromagnetic rumble from the overhead transmission towers than the bottom microphone. The bottom left mic had clean speech buried under the noise. Once we split the stereo channels and discarded the antenna track, we had clean speech. We did not need exotic neural de-reverberation models. We needed basic hardware acoustics.",
  "ACT 5: THE AI-ERA PARALLEL & TERMINAL WORKBENCH [28:00 - 40:00]\nScene: 05. Workbench - Code & Terminal\n\nHere is the lesson for engineering in the AI era. We took the output of the model, threw away the hallucinations, and machined the working parts into a deterministic seven-stage filter in shell and Python. It runs in 40 milliseconds. It muxes the audio back into the master container with zero generational video re-encoding loss. We spent less than a dollar and twenty-five cents in subscription compute, instead of hiring an audio post-production house. Generative models are fantastic at rapid hypothesis synthesis, like a 3D printer in a machine shop. But you cannot leave your software inside a chat window. You have to machine it into deterministic, testable code you own and control.",
  "ACT 6: COMMUNITY CODA, OUTRO & NEXT STREAM [40:00 - 45:00]\nScene: 08. Outro & Next Stream\n\nThat is the spirit of The Sound Above. Across this 37-episode series, we are going to rewatch fifteen years of conversations from the Software Craftsmanship movement, from the early Chicago user groups to the AI horizon, examining how developers tackled architecture, testing, and autonomy before commercial platforms took over. Full transcripts, telemetry, and companion code are open at just3ws.com. If you want to help support our independent hosting and archive preservation, check out patreon.com/whoistechcmty. In Episode 1, we dive into the very first room: Sergio Pereira and Chicago Alt.NET. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and verify your code."
]